WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:05.870
When virtual machines are used, one determination
to make is how the virtual machines will be

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:06.870
networked.

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:11.870
Most hypervisors have some type of virtual
switch manager, and Hyper-V is no exception.

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:14.309
Within Hyper-V, I will select Virtual Switch
Manager.

00:00:14.309 --> 00:00:20.060
Then, I will select New virtual network switch
as I want to set up a testing network for

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:22.390
some new virtual machines.

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:25.289
From here, we have three choices.

00:00:25.289 --> 00:00:30.580
External gives the virtual machines using
this switch access to whatever the host PC

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:36.700
has access to, meaning if the host PC has
internet access, so do the virtual machines

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:38.560
using this switch.

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:43.830
With internal, the virtual machines on this
switch have access to each other and the host

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:45.340
machine, but that is it.

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:51.020
Finally, with a private network, virtual machines
will only have access to each other.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:56.570
Given that a best practice is to keep testing
networks isolated, I will select Private and

00:00:56.570 --> 00:01:03.260
then Create Virtual Switch, name it, Private,
and select the OK button.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:06.890
Switches are then assigned to network adapters
on virtual machines.

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:11.979
To demonstrate this, while I have this Windows
10 desktop off, I will select the virtual

00:01:11.979 --> 00:01:13.460
machine and then Settings.

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:20.140
Then, I will select Network Adapter and Add,
choosing the private virtual switch we just

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:27.109
created, select the OK button, and now the
virtual machine has a network adapter on a

00:01:27.109 --> 00:01:27.960
private network.

